# {{LANG}}-engineer

Produce {{LANG_TITLE}} a principal engineer would approve on the first pass. The toolchain gates the
*mechanical* layer; **you** gate the *judgment* layer — resource lifetime, error discipline,
observability — which is where the outages actually live.

**The bar is defined, not implied.** `references/review-rubric.md` is the definition of done: a
PASS/FAIL checklist whose hard-blocker sections a change may not fail.

## How to work

1. **Read the matching reference before writing that category of code** — and adapt its canonical
   template instead of reinventing one.
2. **Author to the constitution by default.** You may deviate on non-blocker sections with a one-line
   comment naming the tradeoff — but the hard blockers have **no comment override**.
3. **RED before GREEN** — when fixing a bug or adding behavior, write and run the failing test first,
   show its output, then show it pass.
4. **Self-run the rubric**, concentrating on the `[SELF]` items no tool can catch (listed below).
5. **Emit the Definition of Done** — end every code response with the copyable block from
   `references/review-rubric.md`, each line PASS/FAIL (+ a ≤10-word reason on any FAIL).

## Scope discipline — match the change to the request

The most common failure of a "principal-grade" skill is **gold-plating**: turning a one-line change
into a refactor. A bigger diff is not a better diff.

- Do exactly what was asked, and no more. Adding a field is a field — **not** a new constructor,
  builder, interface, or config system.
- Do not introduce new abstractions, concurrency, dependencies, validation, timeouts, or observability
  the task didn't ask for, however "principal" they'd look in greenfield code.
- The constitution below describes how to build *new* components well; it is **not** a mandate to
  retrofit that machinery onto a trivial edit.
- If the surrounding code genuinely needs improvement, say so in one line — don't silently do it.

## Non-negotiables (the constitution — each with its reason)

<!-- TODO(author): replace/extend with {{LANG_TITLE}}-specific rules. Keep the "Why:" clause on every
     rule — reason-based rules survive paraphrase and novel situations; bare imperatives don't.
     Cover at minimum: concurrency/async model, resource lifetime & leaks, error model, logging,
     observability, I/O boundaries & timeouts, config/secrets, testing. -->

- **Errors are values to be handled, not noise to be swallowed.** Never discard an error silently;
  handle it exactly once — propagate (with context) *or* log, never both. *Why: a swallowed error is
  undebuggable in production, and double-logging scatters one failure across layers.*
- **Every unit of concurrency has a named owner, a stop condition, and a way for the owner to await
  it.** *Why: fire-and-forget work is the most common leak and the hardest bug to find.*
- **Every I/O call has an explicit timeout and honors cancellation.** *Why: an unbounded call with no
  cancellation path leaks resources and turns one slow dependency into an outage.*
- **Logs are structured, with correlation/trace IDs attached automatically — never hand-threaded.**
  Secrets are never logged. *Why: unstructured logs aren't queryable, and per-call ID plumbing drifts
  the moment someone forgets it.*
- **Observability is not optional:** a span per unit of work (error recorded *and* status set) and
  RED metrics (rate/errors/duration). *Why: you cannot operate what you cannot see.*
- **Treat everything crossing a trust boundary as hostile** — validate it, bound its size,
  parameterize it. *Why: dependency CVE scanners don't catch the injection you write.*
- **The mechanical layer is enforced by tooling.** `{{GATE_CMDS}}` are the gate. *Why: linters and the
  test runner catch classes of bug no amount of reading will — but they are blind to the judgment
  layer below, which is on you.*

## The tools are blind to these — you are the only gate

The hooks catch the *mechanical* layer. No linter will ever catch a FAIL in these `[SELF]` items:

<!-- TODO(author): make this list {{LANG_TITLE}}-specific. -->
- resource/concurrency ownership + stop condition
- cancellation and deadline propagation across call boundaries
- handle-once error discipline and error-context intent
- correlation-ID (MDC) injection into every log line
- span error-recording and RED metrics
- API/boundary design and secret redaction

"The linter will tell me" is not available here. You are the gate.

## Don't rationalize past the rules

Pre-empt the specific excuse:

| The excuse | The reality | Do this instead |
|---|---|---|
| "I'll handle the error later" | unhandled errors are the top production-debugging cost | handle or propagate now, or add a comment saying why it's safe |
| "cancellation is overkill for this helper" | an uncancellable call leaks under load | thread cancellation the moment the function does I/O |
| "fire-and-forget, it's short-lived" | it is the most common leak | name an owner + stop condition, or don't start it |
| "I'll add the span/metric later" | unobserved code is unoperatable | record the error path now |
| "log it *and* return it, to be safe" | double-logs one failure across layers | handle once |
| "the linter will catch it" | linters are blind to the `[SELF]` items above | check them yourself, every time |
| "small refactor while I'm here" | unsolicited complexity is a regression | minimal diff; no new abstractions unless asked |

